// The GROUP half of the map probe (#4373): eight ctrl tags tested per 8-byte
// load, for the chains too long to walk a byte at a time. Sibling of
// `maps.bit` (hash, descriptor walks, `mapProbe`, `mapGrow`), `mapscalar.bit`
// (the four hot entry points) and `mapcaps.bit` (sizing and scan policy), same
// module.
//
// WHERE THIS RUNS. `mapProbe` -- the `string` and composite-key path -- walks
// bytes for `probeInlineSteps` steps and hands a longer chain to
// `mapProbeSwar`, carrying the index it stopped at and the first tombstone it
// saw. The four SCALAR entry points in mapscalar.bit do the same as of #4394,
// through `mapScalarProbeAt` below, which is this walk with `mapKeyEq`'s
// dispatch replaced by a word compare. Two copies, not one, and mapcaps.bit
// records why: a call inside this loop is what forces `mapProbeSwar` to reload
// `i`, `steps`, `vcap` and `tagRep` from the stack on every iteration, and the
// scalar path cannot afford that.
//
// WHAT THE ACCELERATOR IS ALLOWED TO DO. `mapGroupClean` is true only for eight
// consecutive slots that are all FULL and all carry a tag other than this
// probe's — exactly the slots the byte loop steps over without reading a key,
// setting a tombstone or terminating. Stepping over them eight at a time is
// therefore not a different algorithm, and cannot pick a different slot: the
// probe order, the tombstone rule and the stopping condition below are
// `mapProbe`'s, unchanged.
//
// ALIGNMENT IS NOT A QUESTION HERE, AND THAT IS #4353'S DOING. `loadWord`
// (runtime/gc/mem.bit) MASKS its argument to an 8-byte boundary, so an
// unaligned call to it silently returns a DIFFERENT eight bytes — a wrong tag
// read, which for a map is lost entries rather than a crash. `loadLe8`
// (maps.bit) is a §11.6 `asm` `ldr`/`mov` with no alignment requirement at all,
// hand-verified against `clang -c` + `objdump -d`, and both supported
// architectures permit the unaligned access to Normal memory. So there is no
// `(addr & 7) == 0` fast path and no byte-loop fallback: one code path, at any
// offset. The ctrl array's own alignment is irrelevant and is never asserted --
// `start` is an arbitrary probe index, so seven group loads in eight are
// unaligned by construction. Swapping `loadLe8` for `loadWord` here corrupts
// every one of `_tests_/cases/run_map_swar_scan.bit`'s three key families.
//
// TAIL GROUPS DO NOT EXIST, and the bound below is about the BUFFER, not about
// a partial group. `cap` is a power of two of at least `mapInitCap` = 8
// (mapcaps.bit: `mapCapForHint` doubles from 8, `mapGrow` doubles), so `cap` is
// always a multiple of 8 and the array tiles exactly. What the `i + 8 <= vcap`
// test guards is the WRAPAROUND: a probe near the end of the array would
// otherwise read up to seven bytes past the ctrl allocation, whose contents are
// not ctrl bytes and could report a clean group over an EMPTY slot. Those last
// few slots are walked a byte at a time instead.

// True when the eight ctrl bytes at `w` are all FULL and none of them carries
// the tag broadcast into `tagRep` — the group a probe may step over whole.
//
// THE BOUND IS THE CALLER'S, deliberately: at five IR instructions this fits
// `maxInlineInsts()` (8) and is spliced at its one call site, where a `bl` per
// GROUP would otherwise cost more than the byte steps it replaces. Folding the
// `i + 8 <= vcap` test in here took it over the budget and back out of line.
//
// THE TEST. `x = w ^ tagRep` maps each ctrl byte `c` to `c ^ tag`. Since a tag
// always has bit 7 set (`mapCtrlTag`), for the three slot states:
//
//   c == tag (a candidate)   -> byte is 0x00
//   c EMPTY (0) or TOMB (1)  -> byte is `tag` or `tag^1`, so bit 7 is SET
//   c FULL, other tag        -> bit 7 is CLEAR and the byte is non-zero
//
// so the group is skippable exactly when no byte of `x` has bit 7 set and no
// byte of `x` is zero. `x & swarHigh` answers the first. For the second, the
// textbook `(x - swarOnes) & ~x & swarHigh` is not needed: once every byte of
// `x` is at most 0x7f, `~x`'s high bits are all 1 and the term is redundant, so
// `(x - swarOnes) & swarHigh` suffices. Both are folded into one mask.
//
// EXACT, NOT CONSERVATIVE, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. If the expression is zero then
// every byte of `x` has bit 7 clear — so every slot is FULL — and no byte is
// zero, because a zero byte would borrow to 0xff or 0xfe and set bit 7 of the
// difference. If instead every slot is FULL with a different tag, every byte of
// `x` lies in [1, 0x7f], no subtraction borrows anywhere, each difference byte
// lies in [0, 0x7e], and the expression is zero. The borrow-propagation false
// positive the plain `haszero` idiom carries cannot reach the answer, because a
// borrow can only start at a byte that is already zero — a case in which the
// group is not skippable regardless.
@nosplit fn mapGroupClean(w: u64, tagRep: u64): bool {
  let x = w ^ tagRep
  return ((x | (x - swarOnes)) & swarHigh) == u64(0)
}

// The long-chain half of `mapProbe`, resuming a probe rather than restarting
// it: `start` is the index the caller's byte loop stopped at and `tomb0` the
// first tombstone it saw (-1 for "none", the sentinel `rtMapSet`'s `ins`
// already uses), so the `probeInlineSteps` steps
// already taken are not repeated. Returns exactly what `mapProbe` returns —
// the slot on a hit, `-1 - slot` on a miss, decoded by `mapProbeFound` and
// `mapProbeSlot`.
//
// The byte body below is `mapProbe`'s, line for line. It is a second copy and
// that is the price of the accelerator; both are driven by
// `_tests_/cases/run_map_swar_scan.bit`, which walks chains on both sides of
// `probeInlineSteps` through every entry point, and setting the threshold to 0
// (group scan from the first step) or to 10^9 (never) reproduces that fixture's
// expectation byte for byte.
//
// BOUNDED, though `steps` now advances by 8 as well as by 1: a group is only
// skipped when all eight of its slots are FULL, so the EMPTY slot the load
// factor guarantees can never be skipped over, and the walk stops at it. The
// `steps < vcap` test is the backstop it always was, and the group stride can
// overshoot it by at most 7.
@nosplit fn mapProbeSwar(m: int, key: int, h: u64, start: int, tomb0: int): int {
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  let mask = vcap - 1
  let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
  let keys = loadWord(m + mapKeys)
  let i = start
  let tag = mapCtrlTag(h)
  let tagRep = u64(tag) * swarOnes
  let firstTomb = tomb0
  let steps = 0
  while (steps < vcap) {
    // `&&` short-circuits, so the group load never runs past the end of the
    // ctrl allocation — those last slots are walked a byte at a time.
    if (i + 8 <= vcap && mapGroupClean(loadLe8(ctrl + i), tagRep)) {
      i = (i + 8) & mask
      steps = steps + 8
      continue
    }
    let c = loadByte(ctrl + i)
    if (c == ctrlEmpty) {
      if (firstTomb >= 0) {
        return -1 - firstTomb
      }
      return -1 - i
    }
    if (c == tag) {
      if (mapKeyEq(m, loadWord(keys + i * 8), key)) {
        return i
      }
    } else if (c == ctrlTomb) {
      if (firstTomb < 0) {
        firstTomb = i
      }
    }
    i = (i + 1) & mask
    steps = steps + 1
  }
  // Unreachable for the same reason `mapProbe`'s tail is: the load factor
  // guarantees an EMPTY slot. Same non-panicking answer, not a new one.
  return -1 - (vcap - 1)
}

// The SCALAR sibling of `mapProbeSwar`: same walk, same tombstone rule, same
// return encoding, with `mapKeyEq`'s descriptor dispatch replaced by the word
// compare a scalar key IS. Not a micro-optimisation: a call inside this loop
// puts every loop-carried value in a callee-saved register the prologue
// spills, which is why `mapProbeSwar` reloads `i`, `steps`, `vcap` and
// `tagRep` from the stack on every iteration and this one does not.
//
// RESUMES at `start` with `tomb0`, exactly as `mapProbeSwar` does.
@nosplit fn mapScalarProbeAt(m: int, key: int, start: int, tomb0: int): int {
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  let mask = vcap - 1
  let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
  let keys = loadWord(m + mapKeys)
  let i = start
  let tag = mapCtrlTag(scalarHash(u64(key)))
  let tagRep = u64(tag) * swarOnes
  let gmax = vcap - 8
  let firstTomb = tomb0
  let steps = 0
  while (steps < vcap) {
    while (i <= gmax) {
      if (!mapGroupClean(loadLe8(ctrl + i), tagRep)) {
        break
      }
      i = (i + 8) & mask
      steps = steps + 8
    }
    let c = loadByte(ctrl + i)
    if (c == ctrlEmpty) {
      if (firstTomb >= 0) {
        return -1 - firstTomb
      }
      return -1 - i
    }
    if (c == tag) {
      if (loadWord(keys + i * 8) == key) {
        return i
      }
    } else if (c == ctrlTomb) {
      if (firstTomb < 0) {
        firstTomb = i
      }
    }
    i = (i + 1) & mask
    steps = steps + 1
  }
  return -1 - (vcap - 1)
}

// The probe every entry point's SHARED tail takes: the scalar group walk above
// resumed at `start`, or `mapProbe` for a `string` or composite key, which
// ignores `start` because it has its own byte prefix. ONE call site per entry
// point, reached from BOTH the non-scalar branch and the scalar loop's
// fall-through -- that join is what keeps the loop's registers caller-saved.
@nosplit fn mapProbeTail(m: int, key: int, start: int, tomb0: int): int {
  if (loadWord(m + mapKeyDesc) == mapKeyScalar) {
    return mapScalarProbeAt(m, key, start, tomb0)
  }
  return mapProbe(m, key, mapHash(m, key))
}
